# Media Tracker — settings.
#
# Copy this to .env and edit it. Both dockerRun.sh and the server read it, and
# anything already in your environment wins over what is in here, so
#
#   PORT=9000 ./dockerRun.sh
#
# still overrides for one run. .env is gitignored and never enters the image.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- metadata

# The one worth setting. Free, takes two minutes:
#   https://www.themoviedb.org/settings/api
# Without it, old films and TV are well covered but modern film posters are
# not — Wikipedia only carries freely-licensed images.
TMDB_API_KEY=

# Optional. Free tier is 1000 lookups a day: http://www.omdbapi.com/apikey.aspx
# Adds IMDb's own rating and the MPAA rating, second hand.
OMDB_API_KEY=

# ----------------------------------------------------------------- serving

# Host port, and which interface to publish on.
# BIND=127.0.0.1 keeps it off the network entirely.
PORT=8674
BIND=0.0.0.0

# Require ?k=<token> once, then a cookie, for every request. Set one before
# putting this anywhere other than your own machine:
#   MT_TOKEN=$(openssl rand -hex 16)
MT_TOKEN=

# ------------------------------------------------------------------- extras

# Where the library lives, on the host.
# DATA_DIR=./data

# Run with no network at all. Metadata lookups stop; everything else works.
# OFFLINE=1

# Import the .md lists on start. 0 to leave the library alone.
# MT_SEED=1

# Extra pause between titles while filling in details. Calls are already
# paced per provider; raise this if you want to be gentler still.
# MT_ENRICH_DELAY=0.25

# Container limits.
# MEMORY=256m
# CPUS=1.0
